        "passage": "[I] High above noisy avenues in cities such as Singapore, Tokyo, and Ho Chi Minh City, unused concrete rooftops are quietly turning into productive green spaces. [II] Urban rooftop farms use lightweight soil beds or water-based hydroponic trays to grow crisp lettuce, herbs, and cherry tomatoes right where city residents live. [III] By shortening the journey from harvest to dinner table, rooftop agriculture cuts transport emissions and keeps produce fresher for longer. [IV]\n\nBeyond supplying fresh vegetables, rooftop gardens bring surprising benefits to the buildings beneath them. Bare concrete roofs absorb intense afternoon sunlight, raising indoor temperatures and forcing air conditioners to work overtime. A layer of plants and moist soil acts as natural insulation, cooling the top floor by several degrees during heatwaves. During heavy tropical downpours, the soil also absorbs excess rainwater, easing pressure on crowded street drains.\n\n[[Nevertheless, farming on top of high-rise buildings is far from effortless.]] Rooftop growers must carefully calculate weight limits so that older structures are not overloaded. Strong gusts of wind at higher altitudes dry out leaves quickly, requiring windbreaks and automated drip irrigation. In addition, start-up costs for waterproofing and solar-powered pumps can be steep compared with conventional rural farms.\n\nEven with these hurdles, urban planners view rooftop agriculture as a smart investment in resilient cities. Schools and community centres are increasingly turning their roofs into outdoor classrooms where teenagers learn biology, nutrition, and teamwork firsthand. As urban populations continue to grow, looking upward may offer one of the most practical paths toward greener, cooler neighbourhoods.",
