        "passageTitle": "Community-Led Conservation in Historic Old Quarters",
        "passage": "[I] When historic neighbourhoods gain international fame, they often experience an unexpected transformation. [II] Rising property prices and booming souvenir shops gradually push long-time families out of the very streets that gave the district its character. [III] Once local residents leave, an ancient town risks becoming an empty stage set designed purely for weekend tourists. [IV]\n\nTo prevent this \"hollowing out,\" several heritage cities across Asia have shifted toward community-led conservation. Instead of treating old houses solely as untouchable relics, local authorities offer low-interest loans and technical guidance so families can upgrade plumbing and roofs while keeping traditional wooden facades intact. When daily life remains comfortable, younger generations are far more willing to stay in their ancestral homes.\n\n[[Equally crucial is ensuring that residents share fairly in tourism income.]] In Hoi An’s successful craft cooperatives, for example, local households run lantern-making workshops, home-cooked culinary tours, and small guesthouses rather than renting every storefront to outside retail chains. Neighbours also form heritage committees that monitor noise levels after 10 p.m. and report structural cracks before minor wear turns into costly damage.\n\nPreserving bricks and tiles matters, yet architecture without community loses its soul. When the people who remember the stories behind every alleyway remain at the heart of conservation, historic quarters stay alive, welcoming, and genuinely authentic.",
