        "passage": "[I] In densely packed financial districts, finding enough ground space for a new city park is often impossible because land prices are astronomical. [II] Rather than giving up on urban greenery, architects in Milan, Singapore, and Sydney have begun planting trees upward along the outer balconies of tall residential towers. [III] Known as \"vertical forests,\" these living skyscrapers host hundreds of shrubs, climbing vines, and medium-sized trees dozens of storeys above the pavement. [IV]\n\nThe environmental benefits of vertical greenery extend well beyond striking visual appeal. Leaves filter fine dust particles from busy boulevards and absorb carbon dioxide while releasing oxygen. On hot summer afternoons, dense foliage shades glass windows from direct sunlight, cutting indoor cooling demand by up to 30%. Birds and beneficial insects also use the terraced planters as stepping stones across concrete-heavy neighbourhoods.\n\n[[However, keeping a forest alive at 100 metres above street level requires far more engineering than planting a backyard garden.]] Structural teams must reinforce concrete balconies to bear the heavy weight of wet soil and mature trees swaying in high winds. Automated drip-irrigation pipes deliver exact doses of water and liquid nutrients, while trained \"flying gardeners\" descend on ropes twice a year to prune branches safely.\n\nCritics note that luxury vertical-forest apartments remain expensive, raising questions about whether green architecture only serves wealthy buyers. In response, several municipal housing boards are now testing simpler, lower-cost green facades on public flats and schools. As cities grow taller, integrating living ecosystems into building designs may become standard practice rather than a rare architectural showpiece.",
