        "passage": "Movies often portray young entrepreneurs as lone geniuses who lock themselves in a garage for a year and emerge with a flawless invention. In reality, spending months building a secret product before showing it to a single customer is one of the riskiest mistakes a new founder can make. Modern startup coaches instead teach the \"lean\" method, (18) ______.\n\nAt the heart of lean entrepreneurship is the Minimum Viable Product (MVP)—a simple, inexpensive version of an idea designed purely to test assumptions. If a student wants to launch a healthy snack subscription for exam-season teens, (19) ______. Instead, they might sell thirty sample boxes in the schoolyard for two weeks and track which flavours students actually reorder.\n\nHonest customer behaviour reveals truths (20) ______. Friends often say an idea sounds \"amazing\" out of politeness, yet hesitate when asked to spend their own pocket money. (21) ______, watching real buyers react helps founders spot flaws early, adjust prices, or pivot to a better concept before wasting savings.\n\nMost importantly, small experiments take the sting out of being wrong. When a weekend test flops, it is simply useful data rather than a devastating financial disaster. By testing early and listening humbly, (22) ______.",
