        "passage": "[I] Every year, thousands of university clubs and youth incubators celebrate bold new startups promising to cure loneliness, clean plastic from rivers, or democratise tutoring. [II] Passion gives young founders the courage to start when older competitors hesitate. [III] Within eighteen months, however, many idealistic ventures quietly close their doors—not because their mission lacked heart, but because their bank account ran dry. [IV]\n\nFinancial discipline is often the unglamorous secret behind lasting impact. Successful young founders learn early to separate revenue from hype: winning a pitch trophy feels thrilling, yet paying suppliers on time depends on steady customer sales. They track unit costs carefully, resist hiring large teams too soon, and keep emergency cash reserves for slow months.\n\n[[At the same time, chasing short-term profit without clear values carries its own trap.]] When startups cut corners on worker fairness or product safety just to please impatient investors, loyal customers quickly walk away. Durable ventures treat ethics and cash flow not as enemies, but as two wings of the same aircraft.\n\nMentorship networks play a decisive role in helping twenty-somethings walk this tightrope. Experienced advisers remind newcomers when to hold firm on social goals and when to simplify an unprofitable feature. For any young person dreaming of building something meaningful, blending generous vision with practical arithmetic turns fleeting enthusiasm into enduring change.",
